i'm gabby davis and i'm a jewelry  specialist at jared the gallery of  jewelry and today i'm going to be  showing you how i clean both a diamond  engagement ring as well as a beautiful  amethyst statement ring as well  both of these rings here i would call  extreme cases normally there's a lot of  lotions from people who aren't removing  their rings when they're applying their  lotions or their hair products so for  the most part when a ring comes in  needing a cleaning it just more so has  like a hazy look to it or maybe some  slight caked up of soaps and lotions but  hair is definitely not something that i  see every single day  the very first step before i even begin  cleaning it is using the fine tip of the  needle to actually check the stones  looking at this ring here it looks like  it has been at least six months if not  longer since this ring has been cleaned  now with the style of disengagement here  which is called a split shank where it  has these openings along the side it  really has allowed a lot of this hair  and this build up to become caught in  these openings of the ring  so i'm going to start out by using my  tweezers and needle to be able to  extract all of this hair that has gotten  caught up in all of the openings and  crevices of this ring here the guest who  brought in this ring is actually a  hairdresser which really helps explain  why there is such an excessive amount of  hair and products stuck up within this  ring and how it has built up so much  so what i'll do is push the hairs out  from both directions i'll be able to go  in through that split shank in the top  push it down into the ring where i'll be  able to gather it all together with the  tweezers to be able to pull this out  it looks like the reason all of that  hair is kind of staying and clumping  together within the ring is more than  likely due to all the other products  that she comes into contact with her  being a hairdresser like styling creams  and conditioners and everything that  she's using because as i'm pulling out  these hairs they are really like gunked  and caked together it's not just loose  strands of hair they are all compacted  together  not only has it gotten caught up in the  openings where it's actually visible  while it's on her hand but as well as  the part of the ring that's called the  undercarriage so that opening up  underneath between the actual setting  and your finger has become completely  caked up as well  so with the amethyst ring one thing that  i do have to be cautious about is being  a little bit more precise with using the  tweezers or using the needle versus on a  diamond ring is the amethyst is a bit of  a softer stone so i just want to make  sure that i'm not rubbing this metal up  against the stone to risk scratching it  with this amethyst ring and the way that  it's set down into the metal  it doesn't leave much of the stone  exposed up to the top which means there  is a lot of space for things to build up  within this ring it wasn't until i took  the needle actually around the inside  crevices of the ring that i noticed all  of the hair that was coming out of it  so i start with using a toothbrush to  remove the rest of the debris caught up  in the ring and then i realized that i  can't quite get into those tighter  spaces within the ring so i grab a  jewelry cleaning brush which actually  just has really fine hair like bristles  that make it a lot easier to be able to  get into those tight spaces  after checking all the stones and  pulling out all the debris i take it  over to our polishing wheel the abrasive  powders that are in that compound is  what will actually work to remove all of  those surface scratches and leave the  ring with a nice shiny finish so after  polishing it there's a lot of the  remaining compound on the ring so that  is when i'll bring it over to the  ultrasonic cleaner the solution that's  used in the ultrasonic it's mostly water  than just mixed with a mild alkaline  type solution that helps break down the  polishing compound that's on there as  well as any other buildup that's on the  ring the alkaline solution is gentle  enough to be used on the precious metals  like the silver is gold but it's  powerful enough to be able to break down  that polishing compound as well as  shampoos conditioners lotions soaps  other things that are built up onto  jewelry  since the ultrasonic solution is so hot  i am using a pair of tongs to both place  the ring in there and pull it out of the  solution as well  what really helps clean them in the  ultrasonic is not only the high heat of  it but also the  tiny sound waves or vibrations that go  through the liquid which is what helps  break down all of the residue on the  ring  by removing a lot of the buildup before  actually putting it into the ultrasonic  saves time from it having to soak in  there leaving it in there for extended  periods of time can actually cause the  stones to come loose  with the diamond engagement ring i let  this one soak for about three to five  minutes  since it's not quite as durable with  high heat i only let this one soak in  there for about a minute  so now to finish off i'll bring the  rings over to our steamer which is just  high pressured extremely hot water which  is used to get off any of those last  little bits of hair in this case or any  other debris that either myself or the  ultrasonic missed out on  one thing that can happen when you do  have a lot of build up stuck in a ring  is that can actually act like a glue  just because we don't want that pressure  to be so high that it is able to push  out those stones and have them falling  out  so a few tips that i have for keeping  your rings looking good in between  professional cleanings would be to make  sure that you're removing them anytime  you're doing things such as cooking  gardening applying lotions getting into  the shower washing your hands so one  thing that i do recommend is bringing  your jewelry in to have it  professionally inspected and cleaned at  least every six months which is actually  a free service that we offer at all of  our jared locations where we will check  your stones as well as give it a deep  cleaning
 

